04:17.783 --> 04:27.765
[SPEAKER_00]: bar, we start seeing an acceleration in the ground speed. And the trim speed is not that big of a variation. But when we look at parakeets, they

04:27.785 --> 04:35.612
[SPEAKER_00]: just seem to go a whole lot faster. So what is the trim speed that parakeets have? And is Dune Rider a lot more faster than the other

04:35.652 --> 04:44.901
[SPEAKER_00]: parakeets that are on the market by other brands? So it's not a lot faster than the other brands. It's mainly that we can achieve a better glide.

04:45.598 --> 04:54.462
[SPEAKER_01]: and also a worse glide so i think main difference with between a normal paraglider and a parakite is that with a parakite we want to have the best

04:54.682 --> 05:04.265
[SPEAKER_01]: glide when we are at trim so with our hands down And we want to have the worst glide when we have our hands up because then we start to dive. So our

05:04.345 --> 05:13.413
[SPEAKER_01]: trim speed is a little bit higher compared to a normal paraglider. And this is also because we are using different profiles and we are

05:13.433 --> 05:23.962
[SPEAKER_01]: basically having a better glide when we fly a little bit faster. So we can also fly relatively slow, especially on the really big paraglides. But

05:24.082 --> 05:32.765
[SPEAKER_01]: you almost don't do that. You will feel that once you start to fly a paracord, you will always fly it above trim speed because it's just more

05:32.845 --> 05:43.708
[SPEAKER_01]: fun and it stores more energy. And from that point, you can be way more playful with your glider. But yeah, so we can also fly around like 70 or 80

05:44.108 --> 05:54.402
[SPEAKER_01]: kilometers an hour on the dunes. And coming from a turn is easy to do over 100 kilometers an hour, just on our small dunes even. What I'm wondering

05:54.462 --> 06:00.692
[SPEAKER_00]: is because in paragliders, if you wanted to handle those kinds of speeds, you literally had to go to competition wings, like something

06:00.712 --> 06:09.475
[SPEAKER_00]: like two liner performance. or like the top of the line gliders were able to give you those performance as pilots out there. But with parakites,

06:09.616 --> 06:18.901
[SPEAKER_00]: there's one product, and it's just downsizing, which is allowing you to handle larger wind speeds. What is the basic difference in layman

06:18.941 --> 06:27.687
[SPEAKER_00]: terms, like for those people who haven't tried parakites out there? What is the one thing that makes parakites different from paragliders?

06:28.810 --> 06:35.995
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I wouldn't say it's one thing. I would say it's two things. But the most important thing is that we have kite risers. And this is like a

06:36.115 --> 06:44.642
[SPEAKER_01]: pulley system with toggles as well in your hand, but everything is connected. So basically you have your speed system that is built inside

06:44.722 --> 06:53.748
[SPEAKER_01]: your risers. Once you're steering or you're going hands up, for example, you're basically not deforming your trailing edge to steer.

06:53.908 --> 07:01.869
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically changing the angle of attack of your whole wing. And if you only do this on one side, it's basically, it makes you turn or it makes

07:01.909 --> 07:12.652
[SPEAKER_01]: you dive, for example. I think this is one of the biggest changes, but another big factor is the profile. Like we designed those wings more

07:12.772 --> 07:22.495
[SPEAKER_01]: like a kite. It's more like a kite than a paraglider because with a normal paraglider, the focus is really more on performance and stability when

07:22.535 --> 07:33.196
[SPEAKER_01]: you are at trim speed. And for power kites, it's really important to have the best possible stability, even with your lowest angle of attack. So

07:33.236 --> 07:42.436
[SPEAKER_01]: we've focused a lot on that, that you can go full speed, but still have a great stability. I saw this video on one of your Instagram channels

07:42.516 --> 07:53.821
[SPEAKER_00]: which said that if you hit brakes when the paraglider is diving forward, then there's a chance of stalling it or violent behavior. So why does

07:53.861 --> 08:01.304
[SPEAKER_00]: that happen? Because intuitively for me, when I fly paragliders, I've been told that every time the leading edge dives in front of your head,

08:01.865 --> 08:10.213
[SPEAKER_00]: you should... stop it by pulling the brakes and bringing it back to your head so how is this technology different from the usual paragliders

08:10.313 --> 08:20.465
[SPEAKER_00]: where braking is supposedly the safe thing but here going hands up is the safer thing this baffles my mind if you can talk a little bit about this

08:21.298 --> 08:30.684
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, of course. Yeah, so we are making use of a reflex profile and a reflex profile basically acts like a spoiler. I can actually show it to

08:30.704 --> 08:39.890
[SPEAKER_01]: you up here like a small model. So if the wing wants to shoot forward, then on a regular paraglider, it's pitching forward like this and you have to

08:39.951 --> 08:47.976
[SPEAKER_01]: break it so that it will not collapse. With the reflex profile, it's shooting forward, but then the trailing edge is tilting up and it acts

08:48.036 --> 08:58.030
[SPEAKER_01]: like a spoiler and it brings the wing back so it basically it of course it can collapse in really heavy turbulence but most of the times the reflex

08:58.070 --> 09:06.439
[SPEAKER_01]: will make sure that the wing is yeah moving back coming back to the natural position yeah yeah and then the thing that that you were talking

09:06.519 --> 09:15.418
[SPEAKER_01]: about by hitting the brakes once this happens it's not really about stalling the glider it's mainly that it can completely collapsed.

09:15.998 --> 09:25.462
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if the wing shoots forward and you don't let the reflex do its thing and you break just a little bit or just enough that you take out the

09:25.522 --> 09:36.106
[SPEAKER_01]: reflex, the whole wing can suddenly move forward and the whole center of pressure is moving to the back. So that means that there is no pressure in

09:36.146 --> 09:43.707
[SPEAKER_01]: the leading edge anymore and it can just suddenly collapse. So we basically say that Either you do nothing when this happens, when the

09:43.807 --> 09:52.152
[SPEAKER_01]: wing is shooting forward, just do nothing and the reflex will solve it. But once you are in heavy turbulence, I would never advise this. And then

09:52.292 --> 10:01.738
[SPEAKER_01]: you have to active fly, but then do it a lot by heavy pulling and pull much, but not a little bit. Because yeah, that's not a good combination on most

10:01.798 --> 10:13.360
[SPEAKER_01]: parakeets where there is some degree of reflex. So basically reflex activates the safety mechanism, but only up to a point. And if you mess

10:13.420 --> 10:23.703
[SPEAKER_00]: with that small little buffer that reflex is supposed to solve for you, then of course you put yourself in danger. But if you cross that buffer

10:24.243 --> 10:34.207
[SPEAKER_00]: and give it an input where it negates the reflex from the profile, then it is a lot more easier to prevent the surge like a normal paraglider. Is

10:34.227 --> 10:44.914
[SPEAKER_00]: this correct? Yeah, but the funny thing is this search will not happen that often, actually. The search will mainly happen when you break...

10:45.572 --> 10:56.475
[SPEAKER_01]: that much that you take out the reflex and if you don't do this maybe the wing is just moving a bit but yeah even if we come out from a spiral and we

10:56.495 --> 11:07.499
[SPEAKER_01]: don't make a nice exit even then the we've tested this on the on lake garda and we we came out from spiral and then after this the wing is actually it

11:07.539 --> 11:17.003
[SPEAKER_01]: wants to shoot forward but then it stops because yeah the reflex makes it stop so it's It's actually really easy to control those wings. You just

11:17.043 --> 11:26.030
[SPEAKER_01]: have to keep in mind that, okay, if it's really shooting forward, just pull a lot or do nothing. But don't pull to the point where, yeah, you only

11:26.090 --> 11:33.035
[SPEAKER_01]: take out the reflex a little bit because then you don't have the design shape anymore and the reflex is not there anymore. So then it's suddenly

11:33.075 --> 11:41.998
[SPEAKER_01]: like a normal wing. Is there a way to practice this in ground handling? How do you develop the instinct for this? Yeah, I think ground handling

11:42.098 --> 11:51.341
[SPEAKER_01]: is a great way to practice this, like always for all kinds of maneuvers. Even on the ground, the wing is less stable. So while ground handling,

11:51.401 --> 12:00.545
[SPEAKER_01]: because there's not much wing loading on it. So yeah, exactly. Practice this on the ground, especially in turbulent air. If it's turbulent,

12:00.705 --> 12:10.541
[SPEAKER_01]: just go ground handling. And on the other hand, this also tells you a lot on the conditions. for the dunes especially if you should fly or not if

12:10.561 --> 12:19.824
[SPEAKER_01]: you have your hands up and the wing keeps on collapsing you can expect from a reflex wing that it will be super turbulent because you need some

12:19.904 --> 12:29.227
[SPEAKER_01]: pressure in the wing and even if you have a lot of reflex at some point your lines are not rigid so your wing can still come down and towards you even

12:29.307 --> 12:37.554
[SPEAKER_01]: while it's not collapsing What I'm wondering is what kind of collapses are the most common one in paraglides? Because for paragliders,

12:37.694 --> 12:46.639
[SPEAKER_00]: asymmetrics are something that happen pretty often. And we are told to do whatever, opposite brake shift or opposite brake. It's a fairly easy

12:46.859 --> 12:56.223
[SPEAKER_00]: mechanism to try and navigate an asymmetric. I wouldn't say easy, but at least straightforward. But Parakites, on the other hand, do not have a

12:56.263 --> 13:04.665
[SPEAKER_00]: lot of collapse videos out there. And I understand that they might be being flown in laminar conditions in a coast where there is very little

13:05.345 --> 13:14.627
[SPEAKER_00]: possibility to go out and collapse or face the collapses of Parakite. But what is the usual thing that goes wrong in the Parakite technology

13:14.907 --> 13:25.187
[SPEAKER_00]: that needs pilot input to correct? Okay. So regarding the collapses, yeah, a power cut can collapse. And I think this really differs from type

13:25.267 --> 13:34.453
[SPEAKER_01]: and model. But I can only tell you about the dune riders, we are focusing a lot that once it starts to collapse, we want to have this happening from

13:34.493 --> 13:43.211
[SPEAKER_01]: the tip, so from the side, because we prefer that you have only a tip that's collapsing and not directly the whole wing. Another thing is

13:43.251 --> 13:53.557
[SPEAKER_01]: that we try to make it really talkative. So before it's collapsing, the wing will tell you, hey, something is going wrong and you need to start

13:53.617 --> 14:02.923
[SPEAKER_01]: flying more active. If you ignore this, then at some point you feel like some pressure loss and it feels a bit different compared to normal

14:02.943 --> 14:11.612
[SPEAKER_01]: gliders. You feel it more at your hips and your butt because it's also like everything is pulled. So the feedback that you get is different. So

14:11.673 --> 14:21.460
[SPEAKER_01]: we've been testing this. We didn't get any frontals in the test. in the production series we only managed to get some side wingtip collapses

14:21.680 --> 14:31.885
[SPEAKER_01]: and this is in really rough air or for example when we are soaring with the 12 in more than 30 knots and we are flying all the way to the ground where

14:31.905 --> 14:42.250
[SPEAKER_01]: you get all the turbulence from the waves and if you do then don't do anything and you're not active flying then it might be that wingtip can

14:42.270 --> 14:53.139
[SPEAKER_01]: collapse but even then we managed to to keep the keep flying the wing straight so it's not really moving away and it opens up quickly but what

14:53.199 --> 15:03.023
[SPEAKER_01]: is important i think this counts for every parakite on the market is that once you experience a collapse don't keep your hands high. On a regular

15:03.063 --> 15:10.664
[SPEAKER_01]: paraglider, it's good to keep your hands high. It will start to accelerate and it will reinflate. But basically, you can compare it

15:10.704 --> 15:18.926
[SPEAKER_01]: that if you have your hands high on a paraglide, you are pushed full speed. So it's at a very low angle of attack and it's way harder for the

15:19.306 --> 15:29.030
[SPEAKER_01]: glider to open up again. So what works really good is just to do one hard pull down The wing starts to open up again and then bring your hands to

15:29.830 --> 15:40.658
[SPEAKER_01]: glide position. So not directly hands up again. Yeah, so these are things that can happen. And I think what you need to keep in mind of flying

15:40.719 --> 15:49.045
[SPEAKER_01]: parakeets is that in general, you fly way faster compared to other wings. So you have to take this into account. Crash can be way worse

15:49.125 --> 15:59.234
[SPEAKER_01]: because of your speed. And I think the biggest problem in parakiting is that people can get so confident that they think they can always fly in

15:59.294 --> 16:09.132
[SPEAKER_01]: all kinds of conditions and start to push the limits. And then at some point, yeah, there is a limit. And then it gets really nasty. So

16:09.152 --> 16:20.782
[SPEAKER_00]: basically you're telling me that there's a pseudo sense of safety for people who don't understand the amount of risk that is generated and how

16:20.862 --> 16:29.567
[SPEAKER_00]: quickly it is generated. Whereas in paragliders, the learning curve is a lot longer, so you go a bit more slower and it's more intuitive. yeah

16:29.787 --> 16:40.532
[SPEAKER_01]: yeah imagine that you're flying in really high wind speeds and you're flying on a 12 for example everything happens just so much faster and i

16:40.592 --> 16:50.756
[SPEAKER_01]: even got during prototyping some collapses that i felt them coming and i started to act on it and still was too late so still had the collapse but

16:50.776 --> 17:00.223
[SPEAKER_01]: then the your responses is too late and it still solves the problem but You will get the collapse. And if you are flying on the bigger parakeet,

17:00.343 --> 17:10.817
[SPEAKER_01]: it is more gently. Also, when you fly with lower wing loading, the chance of a collapse is bigger. But if you get the collapse, it's less

17:10.857 --> 17:19.115
[SPEAKER_01]: intimidating, basically. What's the learning curve like in this sport? Because in paragliders, of course, there are like very clear cut

17:19.735 --> 17:27.777
[SPEAKER_00]: levels which you can follow. Like you fly an E and A wing straight after the school, you fly it for 50, 100 hours, you're ready to go to a B wing. So

17:27.797 --> 17:36.161
[SPEAKER_00]: there is like very defined steps that any pilot can go out and progress on. But paragliding seems to be a whole new fun way of chasing

17:36.261 --> 17:46.091
[SPEAKER_00]: adrenaline. And the definition of performance versus safety is just downsizing. Like there's no other homologation which gives you that

17:46.111 --> 17:57.678
[SPEAKER_00]: kind of reference point. So how does the progress of a pilot look from your eyes in this sport? This is a really interesting question. I think,

17:58.199 --> 18:07.268
[SPEAKER_01]: so from what I can see, there are two things happening. You have, or yeah, maybe even more, but you have the group of the traditional paraglider

18:07.308 --> 18:18.210
[SPEAKER_01]: pilots and they fly already and then they want to change to a wing, to a parakite. And the other group are mainly paragliders. For example,

18:18.270 --> 18:26.274
[SPEAKER_01]: people that come from other spots like skydivers, kitesurfers. In the Netherlands, it's mainly kitesurfers. Kitesurfers, they switch from

18:26.354 --> 18:36.318
[SPEAKER_01]: kiting to parakiting. And it's quite funny to see because the people that already do kitesurfing, they have control over the parakite

18:36.758 --> 18:45.922
[SPEAKER_01]: almost immediately because they are used to the same system with the depower and things that is also happening on the kite. But they don't

18:46.022 --> 18:54.537
[SPEAKER_01]: have the the feeling and the sense of flying yet and also not in combination with the conditions and flying with other people around

18:55.317 --> 19:03.925
[SPEAKER_01]: and then the other group are the people that are already flying paragliders and so they basically know how to fly and they yeah they are

19:04.005 --> 19:13.254
[SPEAKER_01]: familiar with many situations in the air but they have to get used to a new system so for this group it's it looks that it's sometimes harder

19:14.229 --> 19:23.011
[SPEAKER_01]: Because all the muscle memory is already there, but it's different. For example, once you experience a collapse, it's not happening often, but

19:23.111 --> 19:31.414
[SPEAKER_01]: if it would happen and you keep the same reflex from flying with a normal paraglider, you're doing it wrong. It's the same with starting. If you

19:31.454 --> 19:40.256
[SPEAKER_01]: launch from the dune and you keep your hands high, you will not take off because there is no lift and because it only wants to accelerate. And so

19:40.276 --> 19:50.281
[SPEAKER_01]: these kinds of things, but once you get used to it, it's, super simple and then there's maybe only a really small group that has no experience at

19:50.381 --> 20:01.545
[SPEAKER_01]: all and they start on parakites yeah and i think the learning curve is different for different kind of people some people they can fly in one

20:01.586 --> 20:12.570
[SPEAKER_01]: day and order of other people it's a few weeks or a few months i would say for progressing and stepping up just keep on flying where you feel

20:12.630 --> 20:22.344
[SPEAKER_01]: comfortable with And don't push too far. Don't go over your limits because in this sport, you cannot go over your limits. So do everything

20:22.424 --> 20:31.906
[SPEAKER_01]: as slow as possible and try to understand what you are doing. But from that perspective, it's not really much different than with normal

20:31.946 --> 20:41.528
[SPEAKER_01]: paragliders, I would say. What I'm wondering is that, is it honestly possible to do two sports at the same time, like arakiting and

20:41.608 --> 20:50.829
[SPEAKER_00]: paragliding? Or because... they are intuitively so different that there is a chance that your subconscious mind will not register things

