24:00.635 --> 24:09.622
[SPEAKER_01]: variations. But yeah, I've done it. And some team riders have done it as well. And yeah, if you know what you are doing, it's totally possible.

24:11.543 --> 24:20.066
[SPEAKER_00]: What I'm wondering is that why has nobody put a port harness on a paraglide yet? Like, why do we only see chair harnesses? Because pod

24:20.086 --> 24:27.130
[SPEAKER_00]: harnesses are much more streamlined. And if we are chasing speed and glide, which is the whole idea of parakeets, where you want extra speed

24:27.150 --> 24:33.713
[SPEAKER_00]: and extra glide to increase the fun factor, then why don't we see conventional pod harnesses coming? Except from the part that, okay,

24:33.733 --> 24:40.916
[SPEAKER_00]: you want to do some toe touches and stuff like that. But let's say if you just want proximity soaring on dunes, do you see pod harnesses being a

24:40.956 --> 24:50.569
[SPEAKER_00]: part of the kit as well in time to come? So in the Netherlands, there are some people using this combination of parakite and pod harness. I think

24:50.990 --> 25:01.304
[SPEAKER_01]: it depends on what you want to do. When you're flying with pod harness, you may have one body position and you have to deal with that. If we fly

25:01.344 --> 25:10.787
[SPEAKER_01]: without the port harness, we have more options because we straightened our legs, for example, to get a better glide. But we can also bend our body

25:10.847 --> 25:20.629
[SPEAKER_01]: down or like our legs down and come out of the harness really easily to get like a worse glide. So if we combine this when flying a parakeet, we can

25:20.809 --> 25:29.403
[SPEAKER_01]: easily get the best glide. And when, for example, we want to go up or when we want to have the glide or when we are flying proximity, But if we want to

25:29.443 --> 25:40.411
[SPEAKER_01]: dive, we are coming out of the harness to even get more dives. And this is something that is way harder to do when you fly a pod harness. And another

25:40.451 --> 25:50.418
[SPEAKER_01]: thing is, of course, that we are flying in a lot of wind. And from my experience, when flying with a pod harness on the dunes in a lot of wind,

25:50.919 --> 26:00.043
[SPEAKER_01]: the chance of starting to rotate or getting twisted in a turn or whatever is also high. way higher so it's totally possible from but for me

26:00.664 --> 26:08.833
[SPEAKER_01]: personally it's not that much more fun but for some people that are seeking for the best glide and most lift sure they are using a potholder

26:09.273 --> 26:17.548
[SPEAKER_01]: there's there's one guy from the netherlands this guy is avon and He is mainly flying modified paragliders. So he's building his own kite

26:17.608 --> 26:26.209
[SPEAKER_01]: riser system and then putting them on normal gliders and using it as a parakite in combination with the pothoners. And you can see that he is

26:26.289 --> 26:37.751
[SPEAKER_01]: getting like the most performance from anyone else at the dunes and even being able to sometimes doing three or four spirals down at the small

26:37.791 --> 26:51.100
[SPEAKER_01]: dunes, which is like a few years ago. I think this never happened. So it's indeed a great way to increase your performance, but it's not only about

26:51.441 --> 27:00.870
[SPEAKER_01]: the glide performance. It's also about how playful is the wing, how much can you dive and stuff like that. What wing is he experimenting on, if you

27:00.910 --> 27:11.777
[SPEAKER_00]: don't mind talking about it? Okay. I think he started on like regular race kites. It was R1, I think. And from there on, he started to

27:11.877 --> 27:23.990
[SPEAKER_01]: experiment with D-wings and even competition gliders. So the one he is flying now mainly, I think it's a Nephuk. Yeah, like a triple C glider.

27:24.691 --> 27:35.756
[SPEAKER_00]: Damn. so kite risers on a triple c and that's endless performance basically then because this the glide is insane and what what happens

27:35.776 --> 27:43.983
[SPEAKER_00]: when you put a kite riser on a paraglider what's the difference in behavior what changes i would say mainly you don't have the same

27:44.043 --> 27:53.608
[SPEAKER_00]: stability when having your hands up so flying at the lowest angle of attack You always have to fly way more active when you put kite risers on a

27:53.668 --> 28:04.030
[SPEAKER_01]: normal paraglider. I would say the chance of a collapse is way greater. And it really depends on what kind of glider you put the kite risers on.

28:04.090 --> 28:14.472
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yeah, if you have, I know there are some competition gliders that are having built-in reflex. So on those wings, I think it's easier

28:14.512 --> 28:23.711
[SPEAKER_01]: to put kite risers and make it work. But there are also some wings that are really bad with kite risers and you don't have much speed range. Speed

28:23.751 --> 28:32.755
[SPEAKER_01]: range will be less for sure. And after this, it will just start to collapse. It might be that your brake pressure is way higher. So this

28:32.915 --> 28:42.198
[SPEAKER_01]: also depends on what kind of kite risers you're putting on the wing. And another thing is that... which is really important, but you don't see it

28:42.338 --> 28:51.400
[SPEAKER_01]: usually directly on the wing, is the spacing of your attachment points. So you have, for example, your A, B, C spacing. And the spacing in between

28:51.460 --> 29:01.483
[SPEAKER_01]: those is really important to keep the ratio aligned with your kite risers. So if it deviates too much from the ideal positioning, it means

29:01.543 --> 29:10.833
[SPEAKER_01]: that you will deform your wing way earlier. You will just deform it when you put your hands up or when you put them down at yeah once they are moving

29:10.873 --> 29:21.057
[SPEAKER_00]: away from the perfect twin position so like the thing with kite risers is that they're a bit like longer because my curiosity is that how does a

29:21.157 --> 29:31.561
[SPEAKER_00]: kite rider kite riser change from the regular risers because kites seem to be a lot further away from the athlete or the body when i see a kite

29:31.581 --> 29:39.784
[SPEAKER_00]: harness attached to a kite whereas a paraglider is much shorter like the distance between the pilot and the glider or the lower surface isn't

29:39.804 --> 29:47.187
[SPEAKER_00]: that long so is it just the length the pendulum effect or is it the material which is or as you said the attachment points like what is the

29:47.407 --> 30:00.933
[SPEAKER_00]: major difference between flying kite risers to a surface than a paraglider rises to a surface yeah i think the in in so basically they

30:01.053 --> 30:11.504
[SPEAKER_00]: look really similar but there are many differences so for example the build construction is different a power kite needs to be built way

30:11.524 --> 30:22.229
[SPEAKER_01]: stronger because we will experience higher g-forces we use different materials often i would say a little bit heavier more heavy duty And of

30:22.349 --> 30:29.858
[SPEAKER_01]: course, yeah, the positioning of the pilot is indeed way different. On the parakite, you are pretty close to the canopy. And with the normal

30:29.899 --> 30:32.182
[SPEAKER_01]: kite, it's often between 17 to 21 meters, 22 meters.

30:36.245 --> 30:45.750
[SPEAKER_01]: so that means if you would put like your harness directly to a normal kite that it's deformed you get you don't get the shape of how it should be

30:46.251 --> 30:55.856
[SPEAKER_01]: because the line there is no line length so that means that the normal kite will become like super curved it will have a super high r shape so you

30:55.876 --> 31:05.530
[SPEAKER_01]: would have to modify this first but of course there's another thing which is We built a parakeet. We developed a parakeet. And it's, of

31:05.590 --> 31:13.894
[SPEAKER_01]: course, we call it a parakeet, but it's optimized for flying. So we check all the situations with flying. How is the roll? How is the dive? How does

31:13.914 --> 31:24.396
[SPEAKER_01]: it respond on the brakes? How does it feel? I would say the feeling of how a wing feels is super important. And then you do the same thing when you

31:24.416 --> 31:34.022
[SPEAKER_01]: develop a kite, right? But a kite is not really made for flying. So you check all the stuff that is mainly very important. So in the end, you end

31:34.102 --> 31:44.628
[SPEAKER_01]: up with a totally different product, I would say. The main things that are similar is, I would say, the airfoil looks very similar. And then the

31:45.448 --> 31:55.596
[SPEAKER_01]: system with the depower and the police is something that is super similar. I'm glad you mentioned the manufacturing side of it because I

31:55.616 --> 32:03.428
[SPEAKER_00]: was on your website a little while ago and I saw that you are doing some pretty cool things out there. Just quickly for the reference of our

32:03.488 --> 32:15.433
[SPEAKER_00]: audiences, who are you and how do you make such cool products? Because the SP80 project that is basically powering some world record speed

32:15.473 --> 32:22.255
[SPEAKER_00]: handling. I'm sorry I should have asked this in the beginning of the show, but what has your journey been like in this scenario and what have

32:22.275 --> 32:30.759
[SPEAKER_00]: you been up to so far before Dune Rider came in your life? Yeah, it's funny that you ask this because Dune Rider is basically like a side project

32:31.019 --> 32:40.653
[SPEAKER_01]: still. I love the humble brag over there that this is just a side gig. The real thing is even cooler. I wouldn't say it's cooler. It's something

32:41.034 --> 32:50.778
[SPEAKER_01]: completely different. So what I'm doing mainly in my daily life is designing big kites. So this starts from 25 square meters up to 400

32:51.919 --> 33:02.341
[SPEAKER_01]: square meters at the moment. And you would ask, what can you do with this kind of kites? How strong do you need to be to fly a 400 square meter kite,

33:02.381 --> 33:12.106
[SPEAKER_00]: man? Do you need bodybuilders to pull the risers? You need many bodybuilders. So with that, we can pull like cargo, like big cargo

33:12.147 --> 33:24.629
[SPEAKER_01]: ships. And so my background is a bit in... I started at a company where we were developing kites to generate... electricity yeah with the kite

33:24.729 --> 33:33.194
[SPEAKER_01]: and the ground station and then the kite was flying figures of eight pulling out the tether and then with that we create we were creating

33:33.234 --> 33:41.659
[SPEAKER_01]: electricity and then we depowered the kite at the zenith where it's not pulling much and then it's retrieving the kite again and this pumping

33:41.719 --> 33:52.324
[SPEAKER_01]: cycle yeah makes it that you get electricity but what i learned is that these kind of kites are Having a way different approach because it's not

33:52.444 --> 34:02.393
[SPEAKER_01]: only about the performance, but safety is a really big thing. And the biggest thing is, I would say, is production because it's really hard to

34:02.433 --> 34:09.477
[SPEAKER_01]: produce those kites. There's many things that you need to do differently and also design-wise differently. yeah you're just

34:09.577 --> 34:19.763
[SPEAKER_01]: approaching it in a different way so we use stronger materials we use construction methods that makes the kite way stronger sometimes we

34:19.803 --> 34:29.609
[SPEAKER_01]: have just one single attachment point that needs to handle like around 400 kilos on just one single attachment point yeah and that's a lot and

34:29.629 --> 34:40.056
[SPEAKER_01]: then even if we have this we still need a whole bridle with a lot of support So the big kites I started on were mainly looking like big kite surfing

34:40.096 --> 34:50.950
[SPEAKER_01]: kites. So they were inflatable. But the bridle was due to this high wing loading thing looking way more like a paraglider. So there are like many

34:51.010 --> 35:01.573
[SPEAKER_01]: lines connecting to the canopy. And yeah, from this, I started to gain more experience and I worked already a lot with a factory that can do big

35:01.633 --> 35:09.000
[SPEAKER_01]: kites. And then I started to do, I basically quit my job and then started my own business and starting to do these kinds of projects for multiple

35:09.020 --> 35:18.168
[SPEAKER_01]: companies. companies, because I found like, oh, there are now many more companies that want this and want this technology integrated in what

35:18.208 --> 35:28.056
[SPEAKER_01]: they are doing, but they don't have the experience yet and I can help them with it. So yeah, for example, SB80 is one of those customers. They want

35:28.096 --> 35:37.190
[SPEAKER_01]: to break the speed sailing world record. And instead of using a regular sail, they want to use a kite because it's more efficient in the end. So we

35:37.210 --> 35:47.396
[SPEAKER_01]: developed this together. And yeah, these are the kind of things that I'm doing. And I would say, so what I said about the production, it's way

35:47.456 --> 35:57.902
[SPEAKER_01]: different. It's more advanced. And even looking at the size, there's no space at the normal kite department to make those big kites. So you have

35:57.942 --> 36:06.724
[SPEAKER_01]: to make a combination in the end. And this also makes it possible for me to really go outside of the box because we need to solve different

36:06.764 --> 36:14.789
[SPEAKER_01]: solutions different problems compared to what we have in the kite surfing or paragliding industry and this is what i mainly like to go

36:14.929 --> 36:25.614
[SPEAKER_01]: outside of the box to see what can we try and then then just go for it then solve this issue yeah i mean what i'm wondering is 400 kilometer

36:25.895 --> 36:33.679
[SPEAKER_01]: kilogram for an attachment point what kind of materials do you guys use on this is it similar to what we have in paragliding or is it completely a

36:33.999 --> 36:45.648
[SPEAKER_01]: whole new industry that we are not aware of No, it's still very similar, but it's way thicker, for example. I would say we are trying to smart

36:45.788 --> 36:56.431
[SPEAKER_01]: optimize all the kites. So we are also using just Dyneema materials, for example, but also Dyneema fabrics. And Dyneema fabrics is not really

36:56.491 --> 37:05.731
[SPEAKER_01]: common yet in paragliders or in the kite surfing industry. It's way strong. It's way stronger. And Coming back to the attachment points,

37:06.252 --> 37:14.978
[SPEAKER_01]: yeah, we are testing often all kinds of different construction. And this goes from even the smallest details, like how long should be a

37:15.038 --> 37:25.084
[SPEAKER_01]: splice? How should it be spliced? And how long should be the taper in how to cut the splice? Because those kinds of things have so much influence

37:25.104 --> 37:35.245
[SPEAKER_01]: on construction. the maximum strength of something. And this goes even together with, okay, then we stitch the attachment point in the strut,

37:35.285 --> 37:45.667
[SPEAKER_01]: for example, for the kite. And how do we do this? What kind of thread? How big is the stitch? And how far is the stitch offset from the seam? All

37:45.707 --> 37:56.428
[SPEAKER_01]: those things together you can use to really optimize the product. But other than that, we are also testing a lot new kind of materials. Yeah. Go

37:57.628 --> 38:06.512
[SPEAKER_01]: on. So this is something that we will see soon in the future. I think new kind of materials that are way lighter, way stronger and different

38:06.572 --> 38:15.809
[SPEAKER_01]: production techniques. So not stitching anymore, but also a lot of welding. Yeah, that's what I was actually getting to that as a designer,

38:16.009 --> 38:22.655
[SPEAKER_00]: because most of the paraglider designer have usually worked with paragliders, right? Like they, they have been in paragliding industry

38:22.675 --> 38:29.961
[SPEAKER_00]: for long enough, and they have invested their time, soul and energy in this single sport itself. But you seem to be having quite a diverse

38:30.041 --> 38:37.213
[SPEAKER_00]: portfolio. Like how did the switch happen? Like how did Dune Rider come into your life? What were the origins of this brand from? Making 400

38:38.074 --> 38:46.705
[SPEAKER_00]: square meter kites to just normal power. This seems to be like a side gig, a project that is like baffles my mind because there are other people who

38:46.725 --> 38:55.936
[SPEAKER_00]: would invest their lifetimes in perfecting something and you just went out and made something just when you had like free time. It seems like

38:55.996 --> 39:06.197
[SPEAKER_00]: that at least. Yeah. Yeah. So in my spare time, I am flying as much as possible. And because I am living pretty close to the shore, to the

39:06.317 --> 39:17.045
[SPEAKER_01]: dunes, I can do dunes soaring a lot. And at first I was a team rider at Flair, but I was looking for something more. I had this idea already a

39:17.105 --> 39:27.028
[SPEAKER_01]: long time in my head to increase performance, get more dive, more roll, et cetera. Within my company, i was looking like i need to do i want to do

39:27.089 --> 39:35.993
[SPEAKER_01]: some more r d but what can i do because if i put this effort in big kite technology i cannot do it alone i have to do it together with the company

39:36.413 --> 39:47.958
[SPEAKER_01]: and the r d goes really slowly we can do maybe one or two prototypes each month but then testing those big kites is is way more challenging you

39:47.998 --> 39:58.683
[SPEAKER_01]: cannot test like multiple kites in one day for example So for me, it was like, okay, it needs to be a project that I can do by myself. And it needs to

39:58.723 --> 40:07.790
[SPEAKER_01]: go fast because I like to iterate fast. And that were the main requirements for myself. And then I thought, why I'm not just putting my

40:07.910 --> 40:17.698
[SPEAKER_01]: effort in learning, designing parakeets, because that's what I like doing the most. And it's even an extra option for me to get airborne even

40:17.738 --> 40:25.100
[SPEAKER_01]: more. And then on my own parakeets. So that's how it started. I had the first sketch was already in my computer for more than one year, but I

40:25.340 --> 40:35.265
[SPEAKER_01]: didn't produce it yet because I knew once I started on it, I will keep on doing it. So at some point I ordered just the first sample and

40:35.465 --> 40:45.009
[SPEAKER_01]: unfortunately it came a bit too late. And in the meantime, I got like accident and I broke my back. So for a few months, I was not being able to

40:45.049 --> 40:54.370
[SPEAKER_01]: fly those prototypes myself, but luckily I have Quite some friends that are also flying. So they were flying my prototypes. And I learned a lot

40:54.430 --> 41:03.933
[SPEAKER_01]: from that because I was there every session. I was recording them on video and I got the feedback from them. And then after a few months, I was

41:04.013 --> 41:13.313
[SPEAKER_01]: finally able to fly myself again. And then I figured out, hey, this I want to still change and this and that. And for me, of course, starting this

41:13.373 --> 41:22.479
[SPEAKER_01]: project was way easier because I already have the connection with the factory. So the same factory is also producing a lot of paragliders. So

41:22.539 --> 41:31.204
[SPEAKER_01]: for this, I went to the paraglider department and they were willing to help me. And I told them like, hey, this is just a project, just an R&D

41:31.244 --> 41:40.930
[SPEAKER_01]: project. I don't know if we will go into serial production ever, but... Please help me with those samples. And they were really friendly and

41:40.970 --> 41:49.177
[SPEAKER_01]: they wanted to help. And at some point we realized like, hey, this wing is really becoming a thing and it's checking all the boxes, all the things

41:49.217 --> 41:57.243
[SPEAKER_01]: that we want. And my friends were like, you should bring this to the markets that other people also can fly it. So then I was just trying to

41:57.283 --> 42:07.238
[SPEAKER_01]: figure out, okay, how do I need to start a brand? I never owned a brand before. So this is something completely new to me. And I got like lots of

42:07.338 --> 42:15.463
[SPEAKER_01]: consultancy from different people that are already owning a brand and things like that. Yeah, we got in touch with Air2Cross for the

42:15.923 --> 42:25.909
[SPEAKER_01]: certification for the shock test and load test. Other people wanted to do a video. And that's basically how it started. I was looking for team

42:25.969 --> 42:35.760
[SPEAKER_01]: riders because we also needed to have people in the video and and before before we know yeah we had to launch so we launched in january this year

42:36.221 --> 42:45.067
[SPEAKER_01]: without knowing what to expect even for the factory they didn't know i basically told them like okay we we are opening the pre-orders and this

42:45.127 --> 42:53.734
[SPEAKER_01]: is the amount that you have to produce so i cannot promise you like minimum water or anything and they were like no we can let's do it this way

42:53.914 --> 43:03.979
[SPEAKER_01]: and we see from there yeah and that's and now we are here we We did a few batches already and production is now a continuous thing, even. That's

43:04.039 --> 43:13.347
[SPEAKER_00]: cool, man. Let's let you add your side gig, take off and spread more awesomeness in the world. But tell me a little bit about the design aids

43:13.367 --> 43:20.192
[SPEAKER_00]: that you use, Brian. If I ask you the question that what goes on from drawing broad to the actual production, it's a pretty wide one. But what

43:20.212 --> 43:31.295
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm wondering is that AI is coming up pretty heavily. You have probably been in design design departments for much before this whole, like

43:31.375 --> 43:40.203
[SPEAKER_00]: revolutionary of chat GPTs and everything coming around to us. For you as an industry insider, how have you seen the artificial neural

43:40.223 --> 43:49.846
[SPEAKER_00]: networks come around and offer their assistance to designers like you? How does it work behind the scenes? You think this will play a big role in

43:49.866 --> 43:58.514
[SPEAKER_01]: the future of kite design? Actually, I know this because I'm connected to some people that are working on this, mainly working at the technical

43:58.694 --> 44:09.143
[SPEAKER_01]: university in the Netherlands. And so what will happen is that everything will be more optimized. So we will know better what kind of

44:09.163 --> 44:19.823
[SPEAKER_01]: air balls to use or how should be even the 3D shape of a kite. Everything will be more accurate. So it will be mainly in the future. You just need to

44:19.883 --> 44:29.432
[SPEAKER_01]: have your requirements and you need to know what you want. And this tool will just help you reaching that goal. And in the end, it will save you

44:29.452 --> 44:38.296
[SPEAKER_01]: some prototypes, I think. So this is something that's going on at the moment. And I think this will be combined with our kite design tools. For

44:38.316 --> 44:47.221
[SPEAKER_01]: example, how it works now, we have a separate software for doing our designs. Then we take the output or for example, like every piece of

44:47.361 --> 44:58.523
[SPEAKER_01]: output, we take it to different simulations and we get the result and we have to basically have to guess on how to continue and how to improve it so

44:58.763 --> 45:06.911
[SPEAKER_01]: you do you iterate between those programs all the time and at some point you're like okay this should be something that works and then you build a

45:06.951 --> 45:15.219
[SPEAKER_01]: prototype but you never know for sure because it's really hard to simulate yeah the the whole canopy because it's also moving it's

45:15.279 --> 45:24.645
[SPEAKER_01]: stretching and that's especially in paragliders is in internal airflow and there are some people that that are already into this and

45:24.705 --> 45:35.491
[SPEAKER_01]: they are simulating this but it's still super time consuming and i think in the future this will be just like almost automated easy thing it will

45:35.511 --> 45:46.355
[SPEAKER_01]: be like a tool it will be like a super easy optimal you have any idea how far that day is like until when does the end user like me and the listeners out

45:46.395 --> 45:57.381
[SPEAKER_00]: there are able to reap the benefits of this new revolution this is hard to tell i'm also not that deep into this part of the tools but i guess this

45:57.421 --> 46:04.654
[SPEAKER_00]: will be like five to ten years something like that It will be in combination with everything together. Something that is going on at

46:04.694 --> 46:14.939
[SPEAKER_01]: the moment is not only simulation that is improving or design tools, but production is becoming different. There's new options in production

46:15.479 --> 46:24.723
[SPEAKER_01]: and materials. There will be different type of materials. So all this combined, I think, will make the whole industry look different, way

46:24.763 --> 46:33.308
[SPEAKER_01]: different in a few years. Ten years ago, if we roll back the clock, nobody would have ever imagined that we could or have a fusion between heights

46:33.428 --> 46:41.512
[SPEAKER_00]: and paragliders and come out with parachutes. You as an industry insider who has seen the evolution of sports over a period of time, do you

46:41.532 --> 46:51.718
[SPEAKER_00]: have any visualization of what could be coming in time to come? Yeah, this is really cool to think about, actually. So maybe I can keep the

46:51.758 --> 47:01.040
[SPEAKER_01]: focus a little bit on parachutes because otherwise it would be so wide. But for parachutes, I think we will see different type of parakites. You

47:01.060 --> 47:09.168
[SPEAKER_01]: have the all-rounders that are suitable for everyone. And basically all parakites are like that now. And then we have only the high

47:09.208 --> 47:17.116
[SPEAKER_01]: performance wing. So that's only one other category. I think there will be many more categories just like with normal paragliding, but then

47:17.336 --> 47:27.018
[SPEAKER_01]: optimized for parakiting. And I think the performance and everything will be pushed even more because people are starting to fly those things

47:27.099 --> 47:35.962
[SPEAKER_01]: more. They are learning to fly them. In some years, they are ready for the next thing. For example, now the scraper is for the expert pilot, but

47:36.022 --> 47:44.606
[SPEAKER_01]: this might be different in a few years if there is like many people that are flying already on this level and they might be looking even for

47:45.046 --> 47:53.897
[SPEAKER_01]: something more extreme. Looking at myself and the direction I would like to go, I think I would be the person that keeps pushing the limits and

47:53.957 --> 48:03.526
